            "Ccirc;circle;radius;C,D;0;0;0;0",
            "E;point;circleSlider;Ccirc,280,130",
            "D1;point;extend;C,D,C,D;0;0",
            "E1;point;extend;C,E,C,E;0;0",
            "CD1;line;connect;C,D1",
            "CE1;line;connect;C,E1",
            "CDE;sector;sector;C,E,D;0;0;black",
            "A;point;free;50,180",
            "B;point;free;210,180",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "G;point;cutoff;A,B,C,D",
            // The two result circles (centre A radius CD, centre G radius ED)
            // are part of Joyce's static figure — keep them drawn (black).
            // They still animate on their slide; the slideshow visible-set
            // governs when they show during the walk.
            "Acirc;circle;radius;A,C,D;0;0;black;0",
            "Gcirc;circle;radius;G,D,E;0;0;black;0",
            "F'F;line;bichord;Acirc,Gcirc;0;0",
            "F;point;last;F'F",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F",
            "AGF;sector;sector;A,G,F;0;0;0",
            // Side hover targets.
            "CD;line;connect;C,D;0;0;0",
            "CE;line;connect;C,E;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            // ---- Transfer rig #1: place CD at A (I.2 + I.1), all transitory.
            // Derived points compute at runtime; the zero-color circles draw
            // gold only while highlighted on their slide (the "not shown"
            // circles, shown here). Equilateral on join AC -> apex t1T; then
            // lay off CD beyond C (t1M) and copy that length from A (t1N).
            "t1AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
            "t1Ka;circle;radius;A,A,C;0;0;0;0",
            "t1Kc;circle;radius;C,A,C;0;0;0;0",
            "t1bi;line;bichord;t1Ka,t1Kc;0;0;0",
            "t1T;point;first;t1bi;0;0",
            "t1TCx;point;extend;t1T,C,t1T,C;0;0",
            "t1M;point;cutoff;C,t1TCx,C,D;0;0",
            "t1K3;circle;radius;C,C,D;0;0;0;0",
            "t1TAx;point;extend;t1T,A,t1T,A;0;0",
            "t1N;point;cutoff;t1T,t1TAx,t1T,t1M;0;0",
            "t1K4;circle;radius;t1T,t1T,t1M;0;0;0;0",
            // ---- Transfer rig #2: place ED at G (I.2 + I.1), all transitory.
            "t2GE;line;connect;G,E;0;0;0",
            "t2Kg;circle;radius;G,G,E;0;0;0;0",
            "t2Ke;circle;radius;E,G,E;0;0;0;0",
            "t2bi;line;bichord;t2Kg,t2Ke;0;0;0",
            "t2T;point;first;t2bi;0;0",
            "t2TEx;point;extend;t2T,E,t2T,E;0;0",
            "t2M;point;cutoff;E,t2TEx,E,D;0;0",
            "t2K3;circle;radius;E,E,D;0;0;0;0",
            "t2TGx;point;extend;t2T,G,t2T,G;0;0",
            "t2N;point;cutoff;t2T,t2TGx,t2T,t2M;0;0",
            "t2K4;circle;radius;t2T,t2T,t2M;0;0;0;0"
        ],
        aliases: {
            "ED": "DE"
        },
        slides: [
            { text: "Unless there is some special reason for selecting particular points {D} and {E} on the sides of the angle {C}, they might as well be taken equidistant from {C} as Apollonius suggested — then only two distances need to be transferred instead of three.",
              visible: ["CD1","CE1","CDE","AB"],
              highlighted: ["D","E","C"] },
            { text: "In order to make {CE} equal to {CD}, one circle is required.",
              visible: ["CD1","CE1","CDE","AB","Ccirc","CD","CE"],
              highlighted: ["Ccirc","CE","CD"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "Ccirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass } ] } },
            { text: "Next, to transfer the distance {CD} to {A}, an equilateral triangle is built on the join. These circles are shown here transitorily; they are not drawn in the static diagram.",
              visible: ["CD1","CE1","CDE","AB","t1AC","t1Ka","t1Kc"],
              highlighted: ["t1AC","t1Ka","t1Kc"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "t1Ka", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "t1Kc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.1" }] },
            { text: "Two more circles then lay off the length {CD} from {A} — four circles in all for this transfer.",
              visible: ["CD1","CE1","CDE","AB","G","t1K3","t1K4","CD"],
              highlighted: ["t1K3","t1K4","CD"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "t1K3", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "t1K4", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.2" }] },
            { text: "Four more circles transfer {ED} to {G}: again an equilateral triangle on the join,",
              visible: ["CD1","CE1","CDE","AB","G","t2GE","t2Kg","t2Ke"],
              highlighted: ["t2GE","t2Kg","t2Ke"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "t2Kg", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "t2Ke", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.1" }] },
            { text: "and two more circles to lay off the length {ED} from {G}.",
              visible: ["CD1","CE1","CDE","AB","G","t2K3","t2K4","DE"],
              highlighted: ["t2K3","t2K4","DE"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "t2K3", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "t2K4", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.2" }] },
            { text: "Finally, two more circles are required, one with center {A} and radius {CD}, the other with center {G} and radius {ED}. These last two circles meet at the point {F}.",
              visible: ["CD1","CE1","CDE","AB","G","Acirc","Gcirc","F","CD","DE"],
              highlighted: ["Acirc","Gcirc","F"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "Acirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "Gcirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "F",     name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
            { text: "The line {AF} is the other side of the required angle.",
              visible: ["CD1","CE1","CDE","AB","G","F","AF","AGF"],
              highlighted: ["AF","AGF"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "AF",  name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "AGF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "In all there are ten circles and one line that must be drawn; the intermediate lines may be suppressed since they only verify the construction.",
              visible: ["CD1","CE1","CDE","AB","G","F","AF","AGF"],
              highlighted: ["AGF","CDE"] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Guide-construction citations (merged into the shared page lookup).
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.1": "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI1/",
        "I.2": "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI2/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

This construction that moves an angle requires a number of steps involving a straightedge and compass. Unless there is some special reason for selecting particular points {D} and {E} on the sides of the angle {C}, they might as well be taken equidistant from {C} as Apollonius suggested. Then only two distances need to be transferred instead of three.

In order to make {CE} equal to {CD}, one circle is required. Next, in order to transfer the distance {CD} to {A}, four circles (not drawn in the static diagram, but shown transitorily in the slideshow above) are required as per Propositions @I.2 and @I.1, and four more circles (likewise) to transfer {ED} to {G}. Finally, two more circles are required, one with center {A} and radius {CD} (which has been transferred), the other with center {G} and radius {ED}. These last two circles meet at the point {F}, and the line {AF} is the other side of the required angle.

In all there are ten circles and one line that must be drawn. The lines in the intermediate stages may be suppressed as usual since they're only needed to verify the construction is correct.

#### Use of Proposition 23

The construction in this proposition is used in the next one and a couple others in Book I. It is also used frequently in the later books. It is also used frequently in Books III and VI<sup class="fn-ref" id="fnref-1"><a href="#fn-1">1</a></sup> and occasionally in Books IV and XI.

Although it may appear that the triangles are to be in the same plane, that is not necessary. Indeed, the construction in this proposition is used to construct an angle in a different plane in proposition @XI.31.

